"""Head-of-line-blocking policy for the shared light-daemon test lane.

Importable on purpose. `test_runner_client.spl` is a standalone script with a
bare `fn main` and cannot be imported by a spec (documented fn-main collision),
so the DECISION lives here where it can be pinned by a unit spec and the client
keeps only the I/O that reads the queue.

WHY THIS POLICY EXISTS — measured 2026-08-17, structural (module counts, not
wall clock; wall clock on this host is a contention artifact).

`strace -f -e trace=openat` over the SAME spec, counting distinct `src/**.spl`
modules opened by the process that actually runs it:

    direct lane   `simple test <spec> --no-session-daemon --sequential`
                  -> 77 distinct .spl modules
    daemon worker  the command light_daemon.spl:132 spawns per request,
                  `simple run src/app/test_runner_new/test_runner_single.spl
                   <spec> --no-session-daemon --sequential`
                  -> 78 distinct .spl modules
    difference    exactly one file (src/lib/common/string_core.spl)

So the light daemon does NOT hold a warm runner. It forks a fresh child that
pays the full module-load cost on every single request. Its amortization factor
is 1.0 — it saves nothing.

What it does add is serialization. `light_daemon.spl` main() is a single
worker draining a FIFO:

    while i < entries.len():
        handle_request(binary, entries[i])

and `handle_request` blocks inside `process_run_bounded(..., timeout_ms)` for
up to the served spec's entire budget, capped at the 600s protocol maximum. One
slow spec therefore stalls every other client on the machine, and the lane is
process-global (`.build/test_daemon_light/`), shared across concurrent
sessions.

That is the mechanism behind the otherwise paradoxical measurement that a WARM
daemon is slower than a cold one: "cold" means an empty queue, "warm" on a
contended host means a queue with other sessions' specs in it. The cost is
proportional to accumulated queue state, not to anything the spec does.

Policy: a client must never join a queue that can only make it slower than
running the byte-identical child itself. The daemon lane is kept — it owns
request expiry and the killed-worker verdict synthesis — but it is used only
when it is free.

# @req REQ-TESTRUNNER-DAEMON-BACKLOG-BYPASS
"""

# Any pending request at all is at least one full spec runtime of serial
# head-of-line blocking, because the daemon runs exactly one worker. There is
# no depth at which queueing becomes cheaper than the direct lane, so the
# threshold is the smallest one that can exist.
val LIGHT_DAEMON_BACKLOG_BYPASS_THRESHOLD: i64 = 1

fn daemon_backlog_bypass(pending: i64) -> bool:
    """True when a client should skip the daemon and run the spec directly.

    `pending` is the number of unconsumed `.req` files in the daemon's request
    directory. Negative or unknown depths (an unreadable directory) are
    reported as 0 by the caller and take the daemon lane, matching the
    pre-existing behaviour rather than inventing a new failure mode."""
    pending >= LIGHT_DAEMON_BACKLOG_BYPASS_THRESHOLD

fn count_pending_requests(entries: [text]) -> i64:
    """Pending-request count from a raw directory listing.

    Only `.req` names count. The directory also holds `.req.tmp` files from the
    atomic tmp+rename write, and counting those would make a client bypass on
    an in-flight write that is about to become its OWN request."""
    var pending = 0
    for name in entries:
        if name.ends_with(".req"):
            pending = pending + 1
    pending
